<h2>What may have survived</h2>
<p>A case file was opened for everyone arrested on a political charge. Today these files are held by FSB and Interior Ministry archives or have been passed to regional state archives. Dispossessed peasants ("kulaks") and special settlers had personal settler files. After rehabilitation a certificate was issued — one can still be obtained today.</p>

<h2>How to search</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Check the databases.</strong> Open List, Memorial's search, Returned Names, regional memory books. An entry usually gives the year of birth, residence, date of arrest, sentence and a source.</li>
  <li><strong>Search under every surname and spelling</strong> — including the maiden name. Not being in the databases proves nothing: they hold only part of the files.</li>
  <li><strong>Request the file.</strong> Relatives of a rehabilitated person may read the case file and get copies. Write to the regional FSB directorate where the sentence was passed, or, for dispossession and special settlement, to the regional Interior Ministry information centre. Enclose documents proving the relationship. Letters must be in Russian.</li>
  <li><strong>A rehabilitation certificate</strong> is issued by the prosecutor's office or the Interior Ministry, depending on who decided the rehabilitation.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What to keep in the tree</h2>
<p>The database and link, the archival file number, the dates of arrest, sentence and rehabilitation — each as its own event with its source. Write calmly and only what the document says: for the family this may be a painful story.</p>
